WEBVTT

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:14.160
Well, take care, everyone.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:17.700
Azuma-san, Kawachi-san, and Suwabara-san, too...

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:18.910
Take care.

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:19.740
All right!

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:26.170
Furthermore, good luck on being their guide, Senpai.

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:27.170
Yeah.

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:28.590
Also, Kawachi-san...

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:30.220
take this.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:31.010
What’s this?

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:32.930
Oh no! I don’t have my kippu!

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:34.470
In case there’s an emergency,
and you can’t get in touch with us...

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:36.560
Call them tickets will you?

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:39.510
If you’re truly in trouble, please open this.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.020
If I’m truly in trouble...?

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:46.400
There’s English bread, German bread, and French bread,

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.320
but Japan’s bread, <i>Japan</i>, does not exist.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:50.570
In that case, there’s no choice but to create it.

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:53.980
This story is a serious, biographical ballad of a boy who possesses Solar Hands,

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:59.200
Azuma Kazuma, who will create a Japanese bread made by and for the Japanese people,

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:02.800
which can be presented to the world proudly.

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:36.690
And now, it’s France!!

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:38.400
Aim for the Monaco Cup!

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:42.010
See ya!

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:43.220
We’re off!

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:43.850
Azuma-san gave us his prize money in its entirety.

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:45.480
Yo! Come quickly, will you?

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:46.020
Okay!

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:47.850
Kawachi-san gave us

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:51.360
a part of his prize money, even
though there’d be less to send to his family.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.120
Kinoshita-san and Manager...

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:56.030
did their best, too.

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:57.360
But...

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:58.530
we still don’t have enough.

00:02:58.530 --> 00:02:59.790
Tsukino-san...

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:03.500
your cooperation is really indispensable.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:10.260
Is it really all right?

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:12.290
It’s for Pantasia.

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:13.750
I’ve made my resolve as well.

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:16.210
Give it to Manager.

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:23.760
Thank you.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.980
We somehow managed to collect this much.

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:31.670
But...

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:33.740
for ten billion,

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:36.300
we need the odds to be almost 200 to 1.

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:39.170
In the end, Kawachi-san...

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:42.820
this gamble depends on how well you play the clown.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.530
The Ten Billion Yen Clown, eh?

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.170
I understand that it’s important to

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:51.800
increase the odds by pretending that
the Japanese team is weak...

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:53.540
But, why does it have to be me...?

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:55.390
What is it, Kawachi?

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:56.810
You don’t look very happy.

00:03:56.810 --> 00:03:57.770
Huh?

00:03:57.770 --> 00:03:59.800
Are you feeling airsick?

00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:02.020
We have a long way ahead of us.

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:04.400
So, tell us immediately if you feel sick.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:06.110
More importantly, Kuro-yan...

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:09.110
More importantly...? Kuro-yan?

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:11.160
I’m not going to worry about you anymore.

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:16.270
We’re going to be entering the Monaco Cup, right?

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:18.460
How do you know about that?

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:20.420
It’s been like that since last year, hasn’t it?

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:22.500
So, of course we’d know about it.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:24.820
Though we actually heard about it from Kanmuri...

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:30.000
It’s true that after your month-long training in Paris,

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:30.830
you’ll be taken to Monaco.

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:33.380
The Monaco Grand Casino

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:37.800
will be hosting a World Bread
Competition for bakers under the age of 22.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:40.210
You’re to participate in this Monaco Cup.

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:42.760
We wish for you to gain valuable experience

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:45.440
by entering into a world competition as newcomers.

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:50.850
This was introduced as an idea from the
General Manager, Kirisaki, as of last year.

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:57.110
However, due to the dishonorable results
of our failing in the preliminaries last year,

00:04:57.110 --> 00:04:58.980
we hadn’t initially planned on participating this year.

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:02.440
But, since you newcomers were truly exceptional,

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:05.200
we decided to participate a second time.

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:08.370
Therefore, we have great expectations of you.

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:09.410
Good luck.

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:12.870
We need to participate as a team in the Monaco Cup.

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:15.420
If Kanmuri had come, he’d have been a great advantage for our team.

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:17.420
To think that you came, in his stead...

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:19.130
What did you say?!

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:21.420
Have you forgotten that you tied with me?

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:25.430
The man I tied with did not have that hairstyle.

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:26.640
I may have changed my hairstyle,

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:28.930
but my face’s the same, so there
should be no problem recognizing me!

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:29.760
You big moron!

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:31.180
A moron?!

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:34.270
You’re the moron!

00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:35.560
No, you’re the moron!

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:36.270
You big moron!

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:37.400
What do you mean, a moron?!

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:38.520
You’re both morons.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:43.230
Huh?

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:58.260
What are you...?!

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:02.960
Kawachi, you and your stupid tricks!

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:04.710
I’m over here!

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:08.220
W-What the hell is this?!

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:12.020
To attack me with a bird... You coward!

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:13.810
It’s not my bird!

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:15.190
You think you’re all that big,
just because you have a bird?!
Sir, please be quiet.

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:15.640
I don’t know what you’re talking about.
Sir, please be quiet.

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:17.230
You’re bothering the other passengers.
I don’t know what you’re talking about.

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:19.400
Damn you, Kawachi!

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:20.940
How dare you insult me!

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:21.570
Step outside, Baldy.

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:22.280
As you wish.

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:23.480
Don’t wish it, please!

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:24.820
And I’m not bald!

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:30.410
Is this the Arch of Triumph?

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:31.990
Amazing! It’s amazing!

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:36.670
It’s the Eiffel Tower!

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:38.790
It’s much higher than the Tsutenkaku Tower in Osaka!

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:43.670
Yay! Champs Élysées.

00:06:43.670 --> 00:06:47.180
Old Man... I mean Mister Kuroyanagi.

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:50.050
I want to sightsee,
even if it’s just for a bit.

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:51.760
This is why countryside hicks are...

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:53.680
You’re also a countryside hick, aren’t you?!

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:54.930
Yep, I am.

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:57.610
That’s why I want to go sightseeing, too!

00:06:58.690 --> 00:06:59.390
Very well.

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:03.480
Getting all excited...

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:05.400
We haven’t come here to play.

00:07:06.070 --> 00:07:07.950
Oh well, don’t say that.

00:07:07.950 --> 00:07:10.030
Starting tomorrow, we begin the serious training.

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:12.240
Let them have fun, just for today.

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:13.420
I guess...

00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:15.040
Wait...

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:17.080
You don’t seem like yourself.

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:19.120
Aren’t you being a little too agreeable?

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:20.340
You’re imagining things.

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:23.800
Sightseeing! Sightseeing!

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:26.350
Yay! Yay!

00:07:27.170 --> 00:07:29.180
Yahoo!

00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:30.510
Could that be...?

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:31.600
Coo?

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:34.310
Kawachi!

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:36.600
What’s that palace-like building over there?

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:37.640
Wait one moment.

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:38.560
In that case...

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:42.310
Hmm... let’s see...

00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:44.310
Those Japanese people are...

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:46.610
That’s the Opera House!

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:49.360
Excuse me.

00:07:57.870 --> 00:07:59.870
This isn’t funny!

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:04.130
What happened?

00:08:04.130 --> 00:08:06.380
Apparently, he dropped his wallet yesterday.

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:08.670
The mornings in Paris are cold.

00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:10.090
Let’s go to the training institute right away.

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:12.720
Things look pretty cold with Kawachi’s wallet, too.

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:14.390
It’s <i>not</i> funny!

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:16.520
This is it.

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:19.390
This is where we’ll be training...?

00:08:19.850 --> 00:08:21.480
It’s really run down.

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:24.400
It’s even smaller than the Southern Tokyo Branch.

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:26.440
You’re late.

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:27.860
Oh, we’re sorry.

00:08:28.490 --> 00:08:30.570
Our newcomer dropped his wallet,

00:08:30.570 --> 00:08:31.950
so we got a little...

00:08:31.950 --> 00:08:33.610
Wallet?

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:35.700
By wallet, do you mean...

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:37.540
this worn out thing here?

00:08:37.540 --> 00:08:38.870
Oh!

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:42.580
It’s my wallet!

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:43.670
You picked it up for me?!

00:08:43.670 --> 00:08:45.420
Really s’il vous plaît!

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:49.260
This is why the Japanese are...

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:50.920
You have absolutely no sense for impending crises.

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:53.420
I stole it. From your pocket.

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:54.800
What was that?!

00:08:55.220 --> 00:08:56.890
Don’t get upset.

00:08:56.890 --> 00:08:58.640
It was just a form of greeting.

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:02.310
You could call it more training
for your month-long stay in Paris.

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:06.860
Public order in Paris isn’t as
good as it is in Tokyo, little boy.

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:08.560
Keep your wits about you,
and take care.

00:09:09.730 --> 00:09:11.820
Who the hell are you?

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:13.990
How did you know that we’re
students at the training center?

00:09:13.990 --> 00:09:16.570
Because you were together with that peacock.

00:09:16.570 --> 00:09:18.120
That’s Coo, isn’t it?

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:19.740
My brother’s pet?

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:21.040
Your brother?!

00:09:22.790 --> 00:09:24.120
Let me introduce you.

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:27.840
She’ll be taking care of your training for a month.

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:30.130
This is Sophie Balzac Kirisaki.

00:09:30.130 --> 00:09:31.590
K-Kirisaki?!

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:36.140
In addition to being an
employee of this store, Gordon Blue,

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:38.590
she’s also General Manager Kirisaki’s younger sister.

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:42.800
M-Meister’s younger sister?!

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:45.560
Pleased to meet you.

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:46.900
Everyone.

00:09:46.900 --> 00:09:48.100
Be sure that you’re not rude.

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:51.070
It’s her who was being rude.

00:09:51.070 --> 00:09:54.490
Moreover, her brother is so very polite...

00:09:54.490 --> 00:09:54.820
Come on in.

00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:56.820
yet the sister picks your pocket as a greeting?

00:09:58.030 --> 00:10:00.570
I wonder if my sister’s all right...

00:10:01.830 --> 00:10:04.120
Oh! This bread is...

00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:05.620
The color’s great, the glaze’s great!

00:10:05.620 --> 00:10:08.000
You can tell at first sight that
everything’s really quite top class.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:11.130
This is all bread that I’ve baked by myself.

00:10:11.130 --> 00:10:12.590
You’re quite skilled.

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:16.590
I guess that much is expected of
the General Manager’s little sister.

00:10:18.430 --> 00:10:20.350
What is this? A croissant?

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:22.100
It’s not shaped like a crescent moon.

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:24.850
You didn’t even know that?

00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:27.920
There are two varieties of croissants in France.

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:30.900
The croissant with the curved shape, seen in Japan,

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:32.570
is made with margarine here.

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:37.070
The ones shaped straight like
these are made with butter.

00:10:37.070 --> 00:10:38.750
That’s the rule.

00:10:40.330 --> 00:10:41.160
In other words,

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:43.390
you can tell simply from the outward appearance

00:10:43.390 --> 00:10:46.870
whether it’s been made with margarine or butter.

00:10:46.870 --> 00:10:49.500
So, French people who come to Japan

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:54.260
probably think that all the croissants
there are made only with margarine...

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:56.550
Depending on the country,
things can be quite different, right?

00:10:57.090 --> 00:11:00.220
There are other differences between Japan and here.

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:02.260
This is very important.

00:11:05.180 --> 00:11:05.760
Try it and see.

00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:09.520
This is Pan de Campagne.
It’s a French country bread.

00:11:10.270 --> 00:11:11.190
This is pretty good.

00:11:11.190 --> 00:11:12.150
Yeah...

00:11:12.150 --> 00:11:14.530
But... it’s a little dry.

00:11:14.530 --> 00:11:15.730
That’s it.

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:19.990
A Japanese person would find this bread a little dry.

00:11:19.990 --> 00:11:23.080
But, since Westerners have
more saliva than the Japanese,

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:25.740
this level of dryness is nothing.

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:29.210
All of the judges at the Monaco
Cup that you’ll be participating in

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:30.500
will be Westerners, of course.

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:33.000
Therefore, you must figure out

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:34.920
how to get over this difference in palate

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:39.510
during your month-long
training here making French breads.

00:11:40.550 --> 00:11:43.020
We’re at a bit of a disadvantage in this battle, then.

00:11:43.810 --> 00:11:45.310
It’s no big deal.

00:11:45.310 --> 00:11:47.510
I’m not worried.

00:11:47.930 --> 00:11:48.940
My brother tells me that

00:11:48.940 --> 00:11:52.270
you’re the kind of outstanding talent
that might appear once every 20 years.

00:11:53.110 --> 00:11:54.400
If that’s true,

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:56.270
then in the end, we’ll be baking the same bread.

00:11:56.270 --> 00:11:59.280
As long as you understand the
tricks to it, it shouldn’t be too hard.

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:00.360
All right!

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:03.280
Then, let’s have you teach us those tricks first off.

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:04.830
Teach us!

00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:07.240
It can’t be helped...
I’ll allow you to teach me.

00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:08.750
The workshop’s this way.

00:12:08.750 --> 00:12:09.880
All right!

00:12:20.590 --> 00:12:22.090
All right, everyone.

00:12:22.090 --> 00:12:24.050
Follow after what I’m doing.

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:31.270
As expected, the way you
knead your dough is first class.

00:12:31.270 --> 00:12:35.440
I guess you were able to pick my pocket so easily because of those nimble fingers of yours.

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:37.740
It’s more like you’re just completely defenseless.

00:12:38.530 --> 00:12:39.990
Oh, Kazuma!

00:12:39.990 --> 00:12:41.820
You pick up things really quickly!

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:43.660
You’re also good, Kai.

00:12:44.950 --> 00:12:48.620
Oh, you know our names.

00:12:48.620 --> 00:12:50.540
That’s because my brother
told me of your defining features.

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:53.540
The guy with the headband is Kazuma.

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:55.540
The guy with the bandanna is Kai.

00:12:55.880 --> 00:12:58.630
I was told that the guy with
the afro would be Kawachi...

00:12:58.630 --> 00:12:59.720
Oh?

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:01.550
Come to think of it...

00:13:01.550 --> 00:13:02.930
Who are you?

00:13:02.930 --> 00:13:04.340
I’m Kawachi! Kawachi!

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:06.050
I changed my hairstyle.

00:13:07.390 --> 00:13:08.930
That sort of thing doesn’t matter one tiny bit!

00:13:08.930 --> 00:13:10.930
“That sort of thing,” you say?!

00:13:10.930 --> 00:13:12.890
Anyone, and everyone...
they’re all annoying!

00:13:13.400 --> 00:13:14.890
There’s something I want to ask you.

00:13:14.890 --> 00:13:15.810
Moreover, my defining feature isn’t my hairstyle!

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:16.820
What?

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:17.400
Moreover, my defining feature isn’t my hairstyle!

00:13:18.110 --> 00:13:20.400
Why is it that, despite having someone of your skill,

00:13:21.030 --> 00:13:24.200
this store doesn’t appear to be flourishing?

00:13:24.990 --> 00:13:26.400
Something like that...

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:29.160
doesn’t really concern you, right?

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:30.410
Of course it does!

00:13:31.540 --> 00:13:35.620
It’s true that we have to learn how
to make French-style bread from you,

00:13:35.620 --> 00:13:38.920
but if your bread doesn’t meet
the taste of the French people,

00:13:38.920 --> 00:13:39.840
there’s no use in us being here.

00:13:40.510 --> 00:13:43.020
It’s natural for us to have such misgivings.

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:44.880
Very well.

00:13:45.140 --> 00:13:46.430
Let’s go out to the front.

00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:48.100
I’ll tell you the reason.

00:13:53.810 --> 00:13:54.390
Look.

00:13:59.730 --> 00:14:02.070
That’s Paris’s most popular bakery,

00:14:02.070 --> 00:14:03.860
Maison Kayser’s New Main Branch.

00:14:04.490 --> 00:14:06.530
With that set up right in front of us,

00:14:06.530 --> 00:14:08.170
there’s nothing we can do.

00:14:08.490 --> 00:14:09.960
Is that so...

00:14:10.330 --> 00:14:13.080
Don’t you think we’ve come across
these circumstances elsewhere, too?

00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:14.120
Yeah...

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:18.580
Starting today, I’ll teach you every skill that I know.

00:14:19.210 --> 00:14:21.590
In exchange, I want you to totally defeat them.

00:14:22.340 --> 00:14:25.980
The Kayser magnates, who’ll be
participating as the French representatives.

00:14:29.850 --> 00:14:31.600
There’s no need for you
to even tell us that.

00:14:32.020 --> 00:14:33.600
I won’t lose to anyone.

00:14:34.180 --> 00:14:35.600
I won’t lose, either.

00:14:36.230 --> 00:14:40.190
Well, either way, under our circumstances,
we need to win the championship as well.

00:14:40.190 --> 00:14:42.240
So, we’ll cooperate with you along the way.

00:14:43.740 --> 00:14:45.740
Thanks, both of you.

00:14:45.740 --> 00:14:46.740
They’re not listening.

00:14:47.410 --> 00:14:48.490
Hello...

00:14:48.490 --> 00:14:49.870
There’s three people here, you know...

00:14:49.870 --> 00:14:51.530
Three people.

00:14:52.870 --> 00:14:55.200
However, reality isn’t that easy.

00:14:56.210 --> 00:14:58.830
Kawachi, you can boil rice, can’t you?

00:14:58.830 --> 00:15:00.210
What’s with the sudden...?

00:15:01.210 --> 00:15:02.550
Yes, of course I can.

00:15:02.550 --> 00:15:05.920
Amongst the Japanese, even an elementary
school child can boil rice, at the very least.

00:15:05.920 --> 00:15:06.970
That’s right.

00:15:06.970 --> 00:15:10.000
Amongst the Japanese, even an elementary
school child can boil rice, at the very least.

00:15:11.180 --> 00:15:12.510
And, similarly,

00:15:12.510 --> 00:15:15.180
amongst the French, even an
elementary school child can bake bread.

00:15:18.020 --> 00:15:23.270
Kayser is a collection of the elite artisans who stand at the summit of the French.

00:15:23.980 --> 00:15:27.400
Of course, they’re all artisans who possess

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:29.500
either Solar Gauntlets or Solar Hands!

00:15:30.530 --> 00:15:33.790
No matter how good you
people are considered to be in Japan,

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:36.410
you can’t win against these opponents that easily.

00:15:37.710 --> 00:15:40.910
For everyone to have the Solar
Hands or Gauntlets... It’s a scam!

00:15:42.250 --> 00:15:43.170
Interesting.

00:15:43.500 --> 00:15:45.210
Follow me, Azuma!

00:15:45.210 --> 00:15:45.880
All right!

00:15:46.340 --> 00:15:47.380
Hey, where are you...?

00:15:48.680 --> 00:15:50.090
Isn’t it obvious?

00:15:50.090 --> 00:15:52.380
We’re going to have a look at the Kayser group.

00:15:52.890 --> 00:15:55.180
I’m going to see if they’re as good as you say they are

00:15:55.180 --> 00:15:56.850
with my own two eyes.

00:15:56.850 --> 00:15:59.440
There can be no disadvantage to
knowing the enemy’s battle capabilities.

00:16:00.770 --> 00:16:02.390
I wonder if that’s truly so...

00:16:02.390 --> 00:16:04.150
If the converse occurs,

00:16:04.150 --> 00:16:06.400
and you lose your confidence after
learning of the enemy’s true strength...

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:08.190
That would be a heavy loss.

00:16:08.190 --> 00:16:10.400
There’s no need for stupid worries.

00:16:10.910 --> 00:16:11.660
Let’s go!

00:16:11.660 --> 00:16:12.400
All right!

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:16.410
Wait please! Me, too!

00:16:21.920 --> 00:16:23.880
As expected of a famous store.

00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:26.760
It’s still before opening hours,
and yet there’s so many people here.

00:16:26.760 --> 00:16:28.670
Oh well, leaving that aside...

00:16:29.420 --> 00:16:32.670
That thing over there that could
be their store sign or symbol...

00:16:33.260 --> 00:16:35.640
Don’t you think it really shows a lack of taste?

00:16:35.640 --> 00:16:37.470
It’s really terrible.

00:16:37.470 --> 00:16:39.360
Talk about being uncool.

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:41.310
Kawachi! Kawachi!

00:16:41.310 --> 00:16:42.150
What is it?

00:16:42.150 --> 00:16:43.690
You think so, too?

00:16:43.940 --> 00:16:46.570
Keep your thoughts to
yourself, Japanese representative.

00:16:47.730 --> 00:16:49.440
He has the same face as the sign!

00:16:49.860 --> 00:16:51.240
Pleased to meet you.

00:16:51.240 --> 00:16:54.450
I’m the third son of the Kayser family, Edward Kayser.

00:16:54.910 --> 00:16:59.250
We’d been expecting the Japanese representatives to arrive soon, and were waiting.

00:16:59.250 --> 00:17:00.450
Look!

00:17:00.790 --> 00:17:01.950
It’s Edward!

00:17:02.670 --> 00:17:03.460
No way!

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:05.460
Really?

00:17:05.460 --> 00:17:06.460
He’s amazing!

00:17:06.840 --> 00:17:08.460
Why are they going “kyaa”...?

00:17:09.670 --> 00:17:11.310
What are you trying to do?

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:14.470
Our eldest brother, Gran Kayser,
has ordered that,

00:17:15.050 --> 00:17:18.220
should the Japanese representatives
come to check us out as they did last year,

00:17:18.890 --> 00:17:23.900
then this year, we would give
them a big welcome as VIPs.

00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:28.230
All right. Then, let’s go and get that big welcome.

00:17:29.480 --> 00:17:31.820
He seems to be a nicer guy
than his mask leads you to believe.

00:17:31.820 --> 00:17:33.610
Yeah, he’s a good guy.

00:17:33.610 --> 00:17:36.490
Why are you taking this so calmly?

00:17:36.490 --> 00:17:38.240
It’s obviously a trap!

00:17:38.740 --> 00:17:41.410
It’s obvious that by showing you how much
of a gap there is between you and them now,

00:17:41.410 --> 00:17:44.750
they’re trying to make you lose your confidence.

00:17:45.750 --> 00:17:47.040
The people who came last year

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:50.380
lost all will to fight just by eating
the bread being sold in the store.

00:17:50.380 --> 00:17:51.970
We’re no good!

00:17:51.970 --> 00:17:53.760
We can’t beat them!

00:17:54.340 --> 00:17:55.810
You’ll be...

00:17:56.260 --> 00:17:58.260
You don’t need to worry about these guys.

00:17:58.930 --> 00:18:00.220
Ryou!

00:18:00.720 --> 00:18:04.020
These guys are made differently than last year’s trash.

00:18:04.020 --> 00:18:06.270
In fact, it just might be...

00:18:07.190 --> 00:18:09.270
them who’d be losing their confidence.

00:18:09.650 --> 00:18:10.770
Oh?

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:13.580
Well then, let’s go.

00:18:16.570 --> 00:18:20.790
The eldest son Gran and
second son Bob are waiting inside.

00:18:20.790 --> 00:18:23.660
Since I’ve heard that this is a collection of elite bakers...

00:18:23.660 --> 00:18:26.830
I’d assumed that they’d
been picked from various places.

00:18:27.290 --> 00:18:28.840
But, with the eldest and second sons here,

00:18:28.840 --> 00:18:30.670
it appears that the administration just favors relatives.

00:18:33.090 --> 00:18:37.060
The administration doesn’t simply
favor relatives, Japanese representative.

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:41.340
We aren’t real siblings. We’re sworn brothers.

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:45.730
We have acknowledged each other as bakers,

00:18:45.730 --> 00:18:48.610
and pledged to aim towards
reaching great heights together, as brothers.

00:18:48.610 --> 00:18:50.190
Sworn brothers, you say?!

00:18:55.150 --> 00:18:56.200
Brother.

00:18:56.200 --> 00:18:58.400
I’ve brought the Japanese representatives.

00:19:16.590 --> 00:19:17.680
Gross!

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:18.800
What are they?!

00:19:18.800 --> 00:19:19.970
Amazing!

00:19:19.970 --> 00:19:22.060
We welcome you, Japanese representatives.

00:19:22.060 --> 00:19:25.720
I stand at the bottom of the two of us,
the Kayser family’s second son, Bob Kayser.

00:19:26.020 --> 00:19:28.060
The man at the top is the eldest son,

00:19:28.060 --> 00:19:29.730
Gran Kayser.

00:19:29.980 --> 00:19:31.860
If is an honor to meet you.

00:19:31.860 --> 00:19:34.730
You think it’s an honor? I think it’s just scary.

00:19:34.730 --> 00:19:39.360
As a symbol of our gratitude for your
having come to visit us from far away...

00:19:39.360 --> 00:19:41.700
we’d like you to try this brioche first.

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:47.250
This is really delicious!

00:19:47.250 --> 00:19:48.410
Oh!

00:19:51.880 --> 00:19:55.380
This is something I thought about
while reading a magazine on the plane

00:19:55.380 --> 00:19:56.380
on the way to France.

00:19:57.010 --> 00:20:01.380
Lately, the story of the activities of a
golden demon appears to be very popular.

00:20:03.180 --> 00:20:04.890
Even if you’re a demon,

00:20:04.890 --> 00:20:07.930
it’s probably not a good idea to
eat yellowtail fish raw like that.

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:09.980
Most kids these days probably

00:20:09.980 --> 00:20:13.660
haven’t seen anything but fish filets in the supermarket.

00:20:14.690 --> 00:20:16.440
There are probably children

00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:19.950
under the impression that fish filets are
found swimming in the sea and the rivers.

00:20:21.740 --> 00:20:25.870
In short, what I’m trying to say is that
children tend to believe what they see,

00:20:25.870 --> 00:20:28.800
and that there’s a risk of them misunderstanding things.

00:20:30.040 --> 00:20:31.630
So, at the very least, I’d like to say

00:20:31.630 --> 00:20:34.460
that you should wash
yellowtail properly before eating it.

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:35.670
In other words...

00:20:35.670 --> 00:20:37.760
Buriwash!

00:20:37.760 --> 00:20:40.180
Scrub scrub scrub scrub...

00:20:40.180 --> 00:20:42.920
Always have fun watching it.

00:20:42.920 --> 00:20:44.350
Amazing.
Always have fun watching it.

00:20:44.350 --> 00:20:44.360
This brioche...
Fun...
Always have fun watching it.

00:20:44.360 --> 00:20:45.810
Fun...
This brioche...

00:20:45.810 --> 00:20:47.430
As expected of Kayser.
Fun...

00:20:47.970 --> 00:20:49.430
These are truly...

00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:51.200
the Goddess’s Hands.

00:20:52.270 --> 00:20:54.530
The results of the technique, La Main De Deesse.

00:20:56.980 --> 00:20:58.030
The Goddess’s Hands?

00:20:58.030 --> 00:20:59.150
That’s right!

00:20:59.610 --> 00:21:03.620
It appears that Solar Hands and
Gauntlets are all the rage in Japan.

00:21:03.620 --> 00:21:06.000
However, it goes without saying that our first class artisans here

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:08.160
have always had something like that.

00:21:08.160 --> 00:21:12.210
In order to knead soft breads like the brioche,

00:21:12.210 --> 00:21:15.670
Solar Hands are not enough.
You need the Goddess’s Hands.

00:21:16.250 --> 00:21:20.630
This comes only to those who have trained
the flexibility of their hands since childhood.

00:21:20.630 --> 00:21:24.220
The suppleness of the joints of these feminine hands and fingers

00:21:24.890 --> 00:21:28.680
is what produces soft and artistic bread.

00:21:29.100 --> 00:21:30.270
So, how is it?

00:21:30.270 --> 00:21:33.100
Do you now understand the
world level, Japanese representatives?

00:21:34.310 --> 00:21:36.190
Are they monsters?!

00:21:36.190 --> 00:21:37.850
How can their hands be so flexible?

00:21:38.650 --> 00:21:40.810
I can do it a little bit as well, but...

00:21:41.950 --> 00:21:43.360
to <i>that</i> extent...

00:21:43.360 --> 00:21:46.120
It’s true that if your fingers are really that flexible,

00:21:46.120 --> 00:21:50.120
you can freely control whether
the dough is soft or anything else.

00:21:50.120 --> 00:21:52.370
So this is the world level...!

00:21:54.250 --> 00:21:55.080
Kawachi!

00:21:55.830 --> 00:21:57.210
This isn’t good...

00:21:57.210 --> 00:21:59.380
At this rate, all of them will lose their confidence...

00:21:59.380 --> 00:22:00.090
Huh!

00:22:00.090 --> 00:22:02.090
That’s quite the farce.

00:22:02.090 --> 00:22:04.840
This thing’s the world level?

00:22:04.840 --> 00:22:06.470
This Japanese representative...

00:22:06.470 --> 00:22:07.840
Has the Goddess’s Hands?!

00:22:07.840 --> 00:22:09.260
That’s right.

00:22:09.260 --> 00:22:11.300
Anyone can do this!

00:22:12.560 --> 00:22:14.060
What is he?!

00:22:14.560 --> 00:22:16.060
Is he human?

00:22:22.070 --> 00:22:24.070
A-Amazing!

00:22:24.950 --> 00:22:26.160
Right, Kawachi?

00:22:26.160 --> 00:22:27.570
You show it to them, too!

00:22:27.570 --> 00:22:28.530
Huh?!

00:22:28.530 --> 00:22:31.000
Umm... Maybe next time.

00:22:32.080 --> 00:22:34.100
Like I could!

00:24:05.520 --> 00:24:07.590
All right, it’s the Monaco Cup!

00:24:07.590 --> 00:24:09.690
Let’s go with all we have!
This is where we have to hang on!

00:24:09.690 --> 00:24:12.260
Kawachi-san, you don’t have to get so excited about it.

00:24:12.260 --> 00:24:13.690
After all, this is broadcast during dinner time.

00:24:13.690 --> 00:24:15.300
What was that?! What do you mean?!

00:24:15.300 --> 00:24:16.740
Next time, on Yakitate!! Japan,

00:24:16.740 --> 00:24:19.070
The critical touch and go situation!!
The prohibited Rodin strategy!

00:24:19.070 --> 00:24:19.970
Be sure to watch!

00:24:20.280 --> 00:24:22.730
Yakitate!! Seed of knowledge.

00:24:22.730 --> 00:24:25.890
In France, the Arch of Triumph stands
towering over the Charles de Gaulle Plaza.

00:24:25.890 --> 00:24:27.770
Wanting to make Paris the number one capital in the world,

00:24:27.770 --> 00:24:29.390
it was built under Emperor Napoleon.

00:24:29.390 --> 00:24:31.740
Its official name is Arc de Triomphe de l’Etoile.

00:24:31.980 --> 00:24:33.950
Oh?!
